# TOPAS 3000A — Rust AsyncAPI Client

Runnable scenario snippets for consuming TOPAS 3000A AMQP 1.0
messages from Rust (edition 2021), using the
[`fe2o3-amqp`](https://github.com/minghuaw/fe2o3-amqp) pure-Rust AMQP
1.0 client and `serde_json` for JSON parsing.

## Layout

- `sdk/` — **not present yet**: a typed Rust SDK is deferred to a
  follow-up sub-project. Each snippet parses incoming JSON
  to `serde_json::Value` or to a small inline `#[derive(Deserialize)]`
  struct; once the SDK ships, swap those for the generated struct.
- `snippets/s1.rs` — subscribe to `{{ channels.detector_measurement }}` and print messages.
- `snippets/s2.rs` — subscribe to `{{ channels.wildcard_fault }}` (all faults across all domains; A8).
- `snippets/s3.rs` — publish a VMS DisplayMessage and await the correlated response (A4 + A6).
- `snippets/s4.rs` — distinguish sync vs delta messages and detect sync-batch completion (A3 + A5).
- `snippets/s5.rs` — subscribe to `{{ channels.traffic_signal_measurement }}` (DATEX-II 2017 profile).
- `snippets/s6.rs` — subscribe to `{{ channels.transport_link_measurement }}` and dispatch on the SCOOT/GPS/ANPR `kind` discriminator (F6).
- `snippets/s7.rs` — consume the static network topology across `network.{node,link,zone,path,turn}` and build five in-memory `HashMap`s.

## Setup

The snippets target **Rust stable (≥1.78)** and need one runtime dep
(`fe2o3-amqp`); JSON parsing uses `serde_json`. tokio is the async
runtime; uuid is used by S3 for correlation IDs; serde supplies the
derive macros.

The canonical, smoke-tested Cargo package lives at
[`asyncapi_clients/smoketest/rust/`](../../smoketest/rust/) — copy
`Cargo.toml` and `Cargo.lock` from there as the starting point. The
package uses seven `[[bin]]` entries pointing at the generated
snippets in place, so one `cargo build --release` produces seven
binaries that share one compile of the dep graph.

Build:

```bash
cd asyncapi_clients/smoketest/rust
cargo build --release   # cold ~2-3 min; warm ~5s
```

Run a single snippet:

```bash
TOPAS_AMQP_URL=amqp://localhost:5672 TOPAS_MAX_MESSAGES=5 \
    target/release/s1
```

## Run

Set `TOPAS_AMQP_URL` to your broker (defaults to
`amqps://localhost:5671`). For local development against an Apache
ActiveMQ Artemis broker:

```bash
export TOPAS_AMQP_URL=amqps://localhost:5671

cargo run --bin s1
cargo run --bin s5
cargo run --bin s7
```

### Environment variables

| Variable | Default | Used by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TOPAS_AMQP_URL` | `amqps://localhost:5671` | all snippets |
| `TOPAS_MAX_MESSAGES` | `5` (S4 uses `20`) | S1, S2, S4, S5, S6 |
| `TOPAS_ORIGINATOR` | `asyncapi-clients-s3-demo` | S3 (sets `{{ headers.originator }}` per A4) |

The names match the Python, Java, C#, C++, TypeScript, and Go
snippets' env vars so the docs stay consistent across languages.

### mTLS

The defaults assume `amqps://` — `fe2o3-amqp` negotiates TLS through
the URL scheme (with the `rustls` or `native-tls` feature enabled on
the crate). To present a client certificate (mTLS), build a
`Connection::builder()` with a custom `tls_connector`:

```rust
use fe2o3_amqp::Connection;
// Build a tokio_rustls::TlsConnector with your client cert and CA
// bundle, then pass it via the connection builder:
let mut connection = Connection::builder()
    .container_id("topas-mtls")
    .tls_connector(tls_connector)
    .open(url.as_str())
    .await?;
```

See the `fe2o3-amqp` and `tokio-rustls` (or `tokio-native-tls`) docs
for the full option set.

## Don't hand-edit

The contents of `snippets/` are generated by
`asyncapi_clients/generator/generate.py`. Re-run with
`make asyncapi-clients` after changing `asyncapi.yaml` or any
template under `asyncapi_clients/templates/rust/`.
